      "label": "a",
      "kind": "working_line",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-\\sin3\\theta}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-(\\sin2\\theta\\cos\\theta+\\cos2\\theta\\sin\\theta)}$ M1"
    },
    {
      "question_id": "IAL2018_WMA13_Oct2022_Q9",
      "label": "a",
      "kind": "working_line",
      "text": "$=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{1-2\\sin^2\\theta-2\\sin\\theta\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin\\theta(1-2\\sin^2\\theta)}$ M1"
    },
    {
      "question_id": "IAL2018_WMA13_Oct2022_Q9",
      "label": "a",
      "kind": "working_line",
      "text": "$=\\frac{1-\\sin^2\\theta}{1-3\\sin\\theta-2\\sin^2\\theta+4\\sin^3\\theta}=\\frac{(1-\\sin\\theta)(1+\\sin\\theta)}{(1-\\sin\\theta)(1-2\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^2\\theta)}$ M1"
    },
    {
      "question_id": "IAL2018_WMA13_Oct2022_Q9",
      "label": "a",
      "kind": "working_line",
      "text": "$=\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-2\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^2\\theta}$ * A1*"
    },
    {
      "question_id": "IAL2018_WMA13_Oct2022_Q9",
      "label": "a",
      "kind": "alternative_method",
      "text": "Example Alt: $\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-\\sin3\\theta}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-(\\sin2\\theta\\cos\\theta+\\cos2\\theta\\sin\\theta)}$ M1; $=\\frac{1-\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta(1-\\sin\\theta)-2\\sin\\theta(1-\\sin^2\\theta)}=\\frac{(1-\\sin\\theta)(1+\\sin\\theta)}{(1-\\sin\\theta)\\cos2\\theta-2\\sin\\theta(1+\\sin\\theta)(1-\\sin\\theta)}$ 3rd M1; $=\\frac{(1-\\sin\\theta)(1+\\sin\\theta)}{(1-\\sin\\theta)(1-2\\sin^2\\theta-2\\sin\\theta-2\\sin^2\\theta)}$ 2nd M1; $=\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-2\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^2\\theta}$ * A1*"
    },
    {
      "question_id": "IAL2018_WMA13_Oct2022_Q9",
      "label": "a",
      "kind": "note",
      "text": "The above approach can be marked via the main scheme but scores the 3rd M before the 2nd M. Alternative approaches will hopefully fit the scheme similarly. Other approaches can be similarly applied as each of the three method marks will generally be required. E.g. see below (allowing first M for students who apply $\\sin3\\theta=3\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^3\\theta$ directly) Note in Alt 2 below it is more likely the 1st M will be gained by aside work to expand $\\sin3\\theta$ with substitution later seen into the equation."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "IAL2018_WMA13_Oct2022_Q9",
      "label": "a",
      "kind": "alternative_method",
      "text": "Alt2 In reverse: $\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-2\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^2\\theta}\\times\\frac{1-\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}=\\frac{1-\\sin^2\\theta}{1-3\\sin\\theta-2\\sin^2\\theta+4\\sin^3\\theta}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{1-3\\sin\\theta-2\\sin^2\\theta+4\\sin^3\\theta}$ 3rd M1; $=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{1-2\\sin^2\\theta-3\\sin\\theta+4\\sin^3\\theta}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-(3\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^3\\theta)}$ 2nd M1; $=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-\\sin\\theta(3-4\\sin^2\\theta)}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-\\sin\\theta(1+2(1-\\sin^2\\theta)-2\\sin^2\\theta)}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-\\sin\\theta(1+2\\cos^2\\theta-(1-\\cos2\\theta))}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-(2\\sin\\theta\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin\\theta\\cos2\\theta)}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-(\\sin2\\theta\\cos\\theta+\\cos2\\theta\\sin\\theta)}$ 1st M1; $=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-\\sin3\\theta}$ * A1*"
    },
    {
      "question_id": "IAL2018_WMA13_Oct2022_Q9",
      "label": "a",
      "kind": "alternative_method",
      "text": "Alt3 By cross multiplying: $\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos2\\theta-\\sin3\\theta}=\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-2\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^2\\theta}\\Leftrightarrow\\cos^2\\theta(1-2\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^2\\theta)=(1+\\sin\\theta)(\\cos2\\theta-\\sin3\\theta)\\Leftrightarrow\\cos^2\\theta(1-2\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^2\\theta)=(1+\\sin\\theta)(\\cos2\\theta-\\sin2\\theta\\cos\\theta-\\cos2\\theta\\sin\\theta)$ M1; $\\Leftrightarrow\\cos^2\\theta(1-2\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^2\\theta)=(1+\\sin\\theta)(1-2\\sin^2\\theta-2\\sin\\theta\\cos^2\\theta-(1-2\\sin^2\\theta)\\sin\\theta)$ M1; $\\Leftrightarrow\\cos^2\\theta-2\\sin^2\\theta\\cos^2\\theta=1-3\\sin^2\\theta+2\\sin^2\\theta(1-\\cos^2\\theta)$ M1; $\\Leftrightarrow\\cos^2\\theta=1-\\sin^2\\theta$ which is true, hence given result is true.* A1*"
    },
    {
      "question_id": "IAL2018_WMA13_Oct2022_Q9",
      "label": "a",
      "kind": "note",
      "text": "Alt3 notes: M1: Applies the compound angle formula to $\\sin3\\theta$ at some stage in the working. Accept $\\pm\\sin\\theta\\cos2\\theta\\pm\\sin2\\theta\\cos\\theta$ Allow for students who apply $\\sin3\\theta=3\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^3\\theta$ directly. May be seen anywhere (e.g. in separate working). M1: Uses correct double angle formula for cos2θ and sin2θ (or sin3θ) to achieve all terms in single angle. (May be before or after cross multiplying.) M1: Cross multiplies and uses Pythagorean identity somewhere in the working in an attempt to achieve a true statement by reaching the same expression on both sides, or reducing to a known true trigonometric identity (as shown in scheme). Award at the stage the identity is applied to an appropriate term in an expression where the angles have been reduced to just θ. A1: Reaches a correct true statement with no errors seen and makes conclusion that the original result is true."
    },
    {
